# Leveraging DB Transactions in Eloquent

Working with relational databases inevitably leads to scenarios where multiple queries must succeed or fail together. If you are accustomed to PDO, wrapping logic in a `try...catch` block and calling `$pdo->beginTransaction()`, `$pdo->commit()`, or `$pdo->rollBack()` is second nature. The challenge often arises when using Eloquent ORM: does this pattern translate seamlessly, or do you need to extend the base model to inject transactional behavior?

The good news is that Laravel handles database transactions natively without requiring any custom model extensions. The underlying connection manager abstracts the raw PDO calls into a fluent interface that integrates directly with your models. To initiate a transaction, you simply call `DB::transaction()` and pass a callable closure containing your logic.

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;

try {
    DB::transaction(function () use ($user, $order) {
        // This entire block runs within a single transaction
        $user->email = 'new@example.com';
        $user->save();

        $order->status = 'paid';
        $order->save();
    });
} catch (\Exception $e) {
    // Any exception rolls back the entire transaction automatically
    throw $e;
}
```

When you enter a `DB::transaction` block, Laravel checks whether an active transaction is already in progress. If one exists, it skips creating a new one and simply executes your code within the current scope. If no transaction is active, it calls `$connection->beginTransaction()` on the underlying connection. Upon successful completion of the closure, Laravel invokes `$connection->commit()`. If any exception bubbles up from within the closure, Laravel catches it and calls `$connection->rollBack()` before re-throwing the error to your `try` block.

This mechanism works regardless of whether you are using SQLite or MySQL with InnoDB. The key requirement for MySQL is that the table must support row-level locking; InnoDB satisfies this by default. If you are running on MyISAM, transactions are not supported regardless of the code you write, and you will face unexpected behavior where errors do not trigger rollbacks as expected.

One subtle pitfall involves how Eloquent handles failures within nested methods. If a model method calls another model's method that throws an exception, the outer transaction catches it correctly. However, if you rely on silent failure patterns or custom exception handling inside models, ensure that exceptions propagate up to the `DB::transaction` scope. Silently catching errors inside a model without re-throwing them will prevent the automatic rollback, leaving your database in an inconsistent state where partial updates have already persisted.

Another common scenario involves running multiple queries on different models within a single transaction. Because Eloquent is just a wrapper around the underlying database connection, all queries executed inside the closure share the same transactional context. This means that even if you are updating a `User` model and deleting a `Log` record, both operations will be rolled back together if either fails.

You can also manually manage transactions if you need more granular control over isolation levels or specific commit points. In those cases, you might retrieve the connection directly:

```php
$connection = DB::connection();
$connection->beginTransaction();

try {
    // Your logic here
    $connection->commit();
} catch (\Exception $e) {
    $connection->rollBack();
    throw $e;
}
```

While this manual approach exists, the `DB::transaction` helper is generally preferred for its readability and automatic cleanup. It reduces boilerplate code and minimizes the risk of forgetting to roll back in an error path.

For teams looking to standardize transactional patterns across a large codebase, consider creating a service class that encapsulates these interactions. This keeps your models clean and ensures consistent error handling strategies without modifying core Eloquent behavior.
